# Secrets access policy

## Never read these files

Do **not** open, read, cat, grep, or otherwise access:

- `.env`, `.env.*` (except `.env.example` which has placeholders only)
- Service-account JSON / credential files (`project-*.json`, `*-credentials.json`, `google-sa.json`)
- Any file that clearly contains API keys, tokens, or private keys

## Never print secrets

- Do not echo, log, or quote values from secret env vars (`GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS_ENC`, `DOC2DOC_SECRETS_KEY`, `GOOGLE_CSE_API_KEY`, `WP_APP_PASSWORD`, etc.)
- When helping configure env, only show **key names** and placeholder values
- Prefer pointing users to edit `.env` themselves

## Allowed alternatives

- Read/update `.env.example` (placeholders only)
- Tell the user which variable names to set
- Use shell checks that only print key **names** (e.g. `grep -E '^[A-Z_]+=' .env | cut -d= -f1`) — never dump values

## If asked to "check .env"

Refuse to open the file. Ask the user to confirm key names are present, or verify via key-name-only commands.
